WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.845
Python input validation requires explicit runtime enforcement.

2
00:00:03.845 --> 00:00:13.526
Type hints, readable models, and framework conventions help developers
understand code, but untrusted data still needs to be checked when it
enters the application.

3
00:00:13.526 --> 00:00:21.903
Python will not automatically make external data safe just because the
code is annotated or because a request came through a familiar framework.

4
00:00:21.903 --> 00:00:24.718
Strong validation starts with clear contracts.

5
00:00:24.718 --> 00:00:34.124
Define expected types, lengths, formats, ranges, required and optional
fields, allowed values, nested structures, and business rules before
processing data.

6
00:00:34.124 --> 00:00:37.694
Normalize carefully, parse safely, and validate by context.

7
00:00:37.694 --> 00:00:44.561
A filename, URL, JSON body, environment variable, command argument, and
CSV row all require different expectations.

8
00:00:44.561 --> 00:00:48.199
Safe libraries and framework validators can reduce mistakes.

9
00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:57.606
Pydantic, Django validators, serializer classes, schema validation,
dataclasses with explicit checks, and safe parsers can all help when used
intentionally.

10
00:00:57.606 --> 00:01:05.090
They should be paired with safe database APIs, safe file handling,
authorization, output encoding, and careful error handling.

11
00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:09.347
Validation is powerful, but it is not a complete security model by itself.

12
00:01:09.347 --> 00:01:12.437
The goal is predictable, safe application behavior.

13
00:01:12.437 --> 00:01:14.840
Test failure cases, not only happy paths.

14
00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:21.500
Include negative tests, boundary tests, malformed input, unexpected
encodings, and business-rule violations.

15
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.216
Log invalid input carefully.

16
00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:32.760
When Python applications validate inputs consistently and verify
assumptions at runtime, they become easier to reason about, easier to
test, and safer to operate.
